---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import CTASection from '../components/CTASection.astro';
import Disclaimer from '../components/Disclaimer.astro';
import { BUSINESS, MEDICARE_2026 } from '../data/site';

const title = 'Medigap vs. Medicare Advantage: How to Choose';
const description = 'Medigap and Medicare Advantage are two different ways to fill the gaps in Original Medicare. Here\'s how they differ, who each suits, and how to decide.';
const today = '2026-06-03';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    { '@type': 'Article', headline: title, description, author: { '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/#agent` }, publisher: { '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/#organization` }, datePublished: today, dateModified: today, mainEntityOfPage: `${BUSINESS.url}/medicare-advantage-vs-medigap` },
    { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': `${BUSINESS.url}/medicare-advantage-vs-medigap#webpage`, name: title, speakable: { '@type': 'SpeakableSpecification', cssSelector: ['.lead', 'h2'] } },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} schema={schema}>
  <PageHero
    title="Medigap vs. Medicare Advantage"
    subtitle="Two different ways to fill the gaps in Original Medicare. Here's how they work, who each suits, and how to choose."
    breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Medicare Plans', href: '/services' }, { label: 'Medigap vs. Medicare Advantage' }]}
  />

  <div class="bg-crst-light border-b border-navy-100">
    <div class="container-wide py-3 flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-center justify-center gap-2 sm:gap-4 text-center">
      <span class="text-sm font-heading text-navy-700">Not sure which path fits you?</span>
      <a href={`tel:${BUSINESS.phoneClean}`} class="btn-accent text-sm px-5 py-2">Call or text {BUSINESS.phone}</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <article class="py-14 lg:py-18">
    <div class="container-narrow prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:font-display prose-headings:text-crst-primary prose-a:text-crst-accent">

      <div class="not-prose mb-8 rounded-xl border-l-4 border-crst-gold bg-navy-50 p-5">
        <p class="text-navy-800 leading-relaxed m-0">
          <strong>Medigap and Medicare Advantage are two different ways to fill the gaps in Original Medicare — and you
          generally choose one or the other, not both.</strong> Medigap pairs with Original Medicare to cover its
          out-of-pocket costs and lets you use any provider that accepts Medicare. Medicare Advantage bundles your coverage
          through a private plan, usually with a network and extra benefits. The right choice depends on how you weigh
          flexibility, predictability, and cost.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="lead text-xl text-navy-700">
        This is one of the biggest decisions in Medicare, and there's no universally "right" answer — only the right answer
        for you. Let's take a look.
      </p>

      <h2>What's the difference between Medigap and Medicare Advantage?</h2>
      <p>
        The simplest way to see it: Medigap <em>supplements</em> Original Medicare, while Medicare Advantage
        <em>replaces</em> how you get your Medicare benefits. With Medigap, you keep Original Medicare and add a policy that
        pays the costs Medicare leaves to you. With Medicare Advantage, a private plan administers your coverage. They take
        opposite approaches to the same goal — protecting you from large medical bills.
      </p>

      <h2>How Medigap (Medicare Supplement Insurance) works</h2>
      <p>
        A Medigap plan works alongside Original Medicare, paying the deductibles, copays, and coinsurance it leaves to you.
        There are no networks and no referrals — you can see any doctor or hospital in the country that accepts Medicare.
        Costs are more predictable, benefits are standardized by law, and the plan doesn't change year to year the way
        Advantage plans can. Medigap does not include drug coverage, so most people add a separate
        <a href="/services/prescription-drug-plans">Part D plan</a>. See our
        <a href="/services/medicare-supplement">Medicare Supplement overview</a> for the details.
      </p>

      <h2>How Medicare Advantage Plans (Part C) work</h2>
      <p>
        Medicare Advantage plans are offered by private insurers approved by Medicare. They usually bundle hospital, medical,
        and often drug coverage into one plan, and many include extras such as dental, vision, or hearing. Premiums are
        frequently low, but you typically pay copays or coinsurance as you use care, up to an annual out-of-pocket maximum.
        Most plans use a provider network, some require referrals, and can change their benefits, costs, and networks each
        year. You can learn more on our <a href="/services/medicare-advantage">Medicare Advantage page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Medigap vs. Medicare Advantage, side by side</h2>

      <div class="not-prose grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6">
        <div class="rounded-xl bg-crst-primary p-5 text-white">
          <h3 class="font-heading font-semibold text-crst-gold mb-3 mt-0">Medicare Supplement Insurance (Medigap)</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-white/85 list-none p-0 m-0">
            <li>• Higher monthly premium, more predictable costs</li>
            <li>• See any provider nationwide that accepts Medicare</li>
            <li>• No networks, no referrals</li>
            <li>• Standardized benefits that remain stable from one year to the next</li>
            <li>• Drug coverage is separate (add Part D)</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-navy-100 bg-navy-50 p-5">
          <h3 class="font-heading font-semibold text-crst-primary mb-3 mt-0">Medicare Advantage Plan</h3>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-navy-600 list-none p-0 m-0">
            <li>• Often a lower monthly premium</li>
            <li>• Bundled coverage, frequently with extras (dental, vision, hearing)</li>
            <li>• Network-based; may need referrals</li>
            <li>• Copays/coinsurance as you go, up to a yearly maximum</li>
            <li>• Benefits and networks can change each year</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Can you switch between Medigap and Medicare Advantage?</h2>
      <p>
        You can, but the rules differ by direction and state. See our guide to
        <a href="/switch-medicare-advantage-to-medigap">switching from Medicare Advantage to Medigap</a> for how that works.
      </p>

      <h2>Where High-Deductible Plan G fits in</h2>
      <p>
        If a Medigap premium feels high but you like the freedom and predictability, there's a middle path:
        <a href="/services/high-deductible-medicare-supplement">High-Deductible Plan G</a> keeps Medigap's any-provider
        freedom at a lower premium, in exchange for a higher annual deductible. It's often overlooked in this comparison,
        and it's worth knowing about before you decide.
      </p>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 rounded-xl bg-crst-primary p-6 text-center">
        <p class="text-white font-heading text-lg mb-2 m-0">Not sure which path fits you?</p>
        <p class="text-white/70 text-sm mb-4 m-0">I'll walk you through both, honestly — no cost, no pressure.</p>
        <a href={`tel:${BUSINESS.phoneClean}`} class="btn-accent text-base px-7 py-3">Call {BUSINESS.phone}</a>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose mt-8">
        <Disclaimer variant="tpmo" />
        <p class="text-xs text-navy-500 mt-3 leading-relaxed">
          This page is educational; specific plan benefits, costs, and networks vary by plan and location and are not
          guaranteed. NY producer license #: {BUSINESS.nyLicense}.
        </p>
        <p class="text-xs text-navy-500 mt-2 leading-relaxed">
          Source: <a href="https://www.medicare.gov" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Medicare.gov</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <CTASection headline="Medigap or Medicare Advantage? Let's find your fit." />
</BaseLayout>
